---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/eng-design-fab/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Sessions 21&ndash;24: Fabrication &amp; Build</h1>
        <p class="text-sn-theme">The Arcade Cabinet Project &mdash; Milestone 4 of 5</p>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <h2>This Is a Work Period, Not a Lesson</h2>
          <p>
            Every skill you need &mdash; CNC operation, additive
            manufacturing, material handling &mdash; already exists from
            the last seven weeks. These four sessions are for turning your
            programmed panels into a physical cabinet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Teams will move at different speeds, especially around machine
            access &mdash; that's expected. A design that looked simple on
            paper often needs a small adjustment once you're actually
            holding the cut panels, which is normal, not a sign you did
            Milestones 2&ndash;3 wrong.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Every Session 4 CNC safety protocol
            still applies, every session, every panel</b> &mdash; certified,
            supervised, one job at a time, no exceptions.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <h3>Cut Your Panels</h3>
          <p>
            Run each panel's job from Milestone 3, rotating through
            available machine time. Follow your pre-cut checklist for every
            single job, not just the first one.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Print Any Additive Components</h3>
          <p>
            For any part you decided to 3D print back in Session 3
            (brackets, bezels, mounts), slice and print it now &mdash;
            using the layer height, infill, and orientation reasoning you
            already worked out.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Assemble the Cabinet</h3>
          <p>
            Join your cut panels per your Milestone 2 design. Troubleshoot
            as you go &mdash; a panel that doesn't quite fit is Build and
            Iterate happening together, exactly as Unit 1 Session 1
            described the design cycle.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Integrate Electronics</h3>
          <p>
            Mount your monitor, install your chosen system (RetroPie
            Raspberry Pi or Windows x86), and wire up your USB controller
            kit and any other hardware from the provided kit.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Spot-Check Timing</h3>
          <p>
            At the Session 23&ndash;24 spot-check, your instructor checks
            every team: is your cabinet physically assembled, and are your
            electronics installed and at least powering on, regardless of
            polish? A team that's badly behind at that point needs to flag it
            now, not wait until the milestone ends.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Optional Stretch Features</h3>
          <p>
            If your team finishes core assembly and electronics with time to
            spare, T-molding and marquee LEDs are available extensions
            &mdash; genuinely optional, not required to demonstrate
            proficiency.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>By the Session 23&ndash;24 spot-check, your instructor confirms:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>All panels are cut and the cabinet is physically assembled</li>
            <li>Electronics are installed and at least powering on</li>
            <li>No CNC safety protocol is being skipped, on any job</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            "Functional" is the bar here, not "finished" or "polished"
            &mdash; that distinction matters, since Milestone 5 needs real
            time to present and reflect, not a squeezed leftover.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these on your own. Reflections are individual, even when the rest of the session was team work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>Did your route-or-print decisions from Session 3 hold up once you were actually building, or would you decide differently now?</li>
            <li>Milestone 5 asks you to present this cabinet to your client. What's the one feature you're most looking forward to explaining &mdash; and the one you're least looking forward to?</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Keep a build log as you go</b> &mdash;
            the date, what changed from your Milestone 2 design, and why.
            It is the only written team deliverable this milestone, and
            Milestone 5's presentation draws on it directly, so fill it in
            while you remember the reason rather than at the end.
          </p>

          <p>
            Beyond the log, this milestone is graded as a hard checkpoint via
            your instructor's direct spot-check, plus your individual
            reflection.
          </p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Milestone Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
